  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday: The heat wave continues as strong mid/upper \nridging builds across the region. High temperatures will again\napproach the triple digits in many areas today and tomorrow, with\nhigh temp records in jeopardy at multiple local climate sites. \nOne notable difference from recent days is a shift to easterly low\nlevel flow, gradually bringing a drier airmass into the area. \nThis will yield lower heat indices across a portion of east\ncentral MS today and for most areas east of I-55 on Wednesday. A \nHeat Advisory remains in effect today for all other areas, and \nanother may be needed tomorrow, though likely for a smaller area \nthan today. With deep layer moisture becoming more limited to the \nsouthern and western portions of the area, that is the region \nwhere diurnal convection will be more likely today - south/west \nMS, northeast LA, and southeast AR. By tomorrow, the focus will be\neven more limited to south MS and central LA. A few of these \nstorms could produce locally strong wind gusts. \n\nThursday through next Tuesday: A drier airmass will continue to\nshift into the area Thursday into Friday. This airmass will still\nsupport hot days, with temps still approaching the triple digits \nin the ArkLaMiss Delta through Saturday. But with dewpoints in \nthe 60s, mixing down into the 50s at times, heat stress will \nsignificantly decrease through the end of the week. Morning temps \nwill also dip into the 60s at times in many areas late this week.\nDeep layer moisture will begin to increase again this weekend \ninto early next week, with returning chances for diurnal \nconvection each day. /DL/",
